## p1-torq | Torque and rotation | physics | 1 | phys1 | p1-mom
explain 2 | Torque: the rotational version of force
- τ = r × F, size rF sin θ.
- Push a door near the hinge and it barely moves. Push the handle: easy.
- *Longer lever arm, same force, more torque.*
> Archimedes: give me a place to stand and I will move the Earth

quiz 3 | A 60 kg child sits 1.5 m from a seesaw's pivot. Where must a 90 kg adult sit to balance?
* 1 m from the pivot
o 1.5 m
o 2.25 m
o 0.5 m
= Torques balance: 60 × 1.5 = 90 × d.
> Rotational second law: τ = Iα

explain 3 | τ = Iα: rotational Newton's second law
- I (moment of inertia) plays the role of mass.
- It depends on how far the mass sits from the axis: I = Σmr².
- *A figure skater can't change their mass, but can change I.*
> Rolling combines spinning and sliding

predict 4 | A solid ball and a hollow ball of equal mass and radius race down a ramp. Which wins?
* The solid ball
o The hollow ball
o Tie
= Solid sphere I = (2/5)mr², hollow (2/3)mr². Less energy goes into spinning the solid one.
> Gyroscopes resist tipping because of angular momentum

## p1-angm | Angular momentum | physics | 1 | phys1 | p1-torq
explain 3 | Angular momentum is conserved without external torque
- L = Iω for a rigid body. L = r × p for a particle.
- Pull mass inward and spin speeds up.
- *Divers tuck to spin fast, then open out to slow down before entering the water.*
> Cats use it to land on their feet

story 4 | How a falling cat turns over without breaking physics
- A cat starting with zero angular momentum must keep zero overall.
- It bends at the middle and twists its front and back halves in opposite directions, tucking legs to change their moments of inertia.
- *Net rotation without net angular momentum.* Physicists published papers on this in 1969.
> Precession: why a spinning top doesn't fall

explain 4 | Precession
- Gravity's torque on a tilted spinning top points sideways, not down.
- So the angular momentum vector swings around in a circle instead of toppling.
- Earth precesses too: its axis traces a circle every ~26,000 years. Polaris won't always be the pole star.
> Kepler's second law is angular momentum conservation

quiz 3 | Why does a planet move fastest when it's closest to the Sun?
* Angular momentum r × mv is conserved
o The Sun pushes it harder
o Energy is lost far from the Sun
o Kepler's third law
= Gravity points toward the Sun: no torque. So rv sin θ stays constant.
= Small r means large v. Equal areas in equal times.
> Next: gravity itself

## p1-grav | Gravitation | physics | 1 | phys1 | p1-angm
explain 2 | Newton's law of gravitation
- $ F = GMm/r²
- G = 6.674 × 10⁻¹¹ N m²/kg²: tiny, which is why gravity between people is undetectable.
- Same law for an apple and the Moon.
> Weighing the Earth took a lead ball experiment

story 4 | Cavendish weighed the Earth in a shed in 1798
- He hung lead balls from a torsion wire and measured the minute twist as bigger balls attracted them.
- That fixed G, and from g at the surface, Earth's mass and density.
- He found Earth is about 5.4 times denser than water: so its core must be heavy.
> g changes with height, but slowly

quiz 3 | At what height above the surface is g a quarter of its surface value?
* One Earth radius up (~6,400 km)
o 100 km
o Two Earth radii up
o It never drops that low
= g ∝ 1/r². Doubling r from the centre quarters g.
> Escape velocity doesn't depend on the launch angle

quiz 3 | Escape velocity from Earth's surface?
* About 11.2 km/s
o About 7.9 km/s
o About 3 km/s
o About 30 km/s
= ½v² = GM/R gives v = √(2GM/R).
= 7.9 km/s is the speed for a low circular orbit: escape is √2 times that.
> Make an object small enough and not even light escapes

story 5 | Black holes were predicted in 1783
- John Michell: a star dense enough would have an escape velocity above the speed of light. It would be invisible.
- Set √(2GM/R) = c: R = 2GM/c², exactly the Schwarzschild radius general relativity gives.
- *Right answer, wrong theory.* Earth would need to be squeezed to about 9 mm.
> Next: things that wobble

## p1-shm | Simple harmonic motion | physics | 1 | phys1 | p1-grav
explain 3 | SHM: acceleration proportional to displacement, pointing back
- a = −ω²x. Solution: x = A cos(ωt + φ).
- Mass on a spring: ω = √(k/m). Pendulum: ω = √(g/L).
- *Period doesn't depend on amplitude.* That's why pendulum clocks keep time.
? Angular frequency of a mass on a spring? :: ω = √(k/m)
> Galileo noticed the period trick in a cathedral

story 3 | Galileo timed a swinging lamp with his pulse
- The story: in Pisa Cathedral, around 1583, Galileo saw a chandelier swing and timed it with his pulse.
- Big swings and small swings seemed to take the same time.
- *Isochronism became the basis of pendulum clocks.* (True only for small swings.)
> Large swings break SHM

quiz 3 | A 1 m pendulum on Earth: period roughly? (g ≈ 9.8)
* 2 s
o 1 s
o 6.3 s
o 0.5 s
= T = 2π√(1/9.8) ≈ 2.0 s.
= The metre was once nearly defined as the length of a pendulum with a 2 s period.
> Energy sloshes between kinetic and potential

explain 2 | Energy in SHM
- At the ends: all potential, zero speed.
- At the centre: all kinetic, maximum speed Aω.
- Total energy ½kA² stays constant.
> Next: friction and pushing

## p1-damp | Damped and driven oscillations | physics | 2 | phys1 | p1-shm
explain 3 | Damping: underdamped, critical, overdamped
- Underdamped: oscillates while decaying. Car with worn shocks.
- Critical: returns fastest without overshoot. Good suspension, door closers.
- Overdamped: oozes back slowly.
> Driving at the natural frequency gives resonance

explain 4 | Quality factor Q
- Q ≈ number of oscillations before the energy drops a lot (precisely: 2π × energy stored / energy lost per cycle).
- A wine glass: Q around 1,000. A quartz watch crystal: around 10,000 or more.
- *High Q: sharp resonance, long ringing.*
> Why quartz watches are so accurate

real 4 | Your watch counts 32,768 vibrations per second
- A tiny quartz tuning fork resonates at 32,768 Hz = 2¹⁵.
- Fifteen halvings in a digital circuit give exactly one tick per second.
- High Q means the frequency barely drifts: a few seconds a month.
> Next: fluids

## p1-fluid | Fluids | physics | 1 | phys1 | p1-damp
explain 2 | Pressure in a fluid increases with depth
- p = p₀ + ρgh.
- Every 10 m of water adds about one atmosphere.
- *Shape of the container doesn't matter*, only depth.
> Archimedes in the bath

story 3 | Archimedes and the crown
- Story: King Hiero suspected his gold crown had silver mixed in.
- Archimedes realised the crown's volume could be found by the water it displaced, then compared with pure gold of the same weight.
- The "Eureka" bath story comes from Vitruvius, two centuries later.
> Buoyancy is the weight of displaced fluid

quiz 3 | An iceberg floats in seawater. Roughly what fraction is underwater?
* About 90%
o About 50%
o About 10%
o It depends on its shape
= Fraction submerged = density of ice / density of seawater ≈ 917/1025 ≈ 0.89.
> Fast fluid means low pressure

explain 3 | Bernoulli's principle
- Along a streamline: p + ½ρv² + ρgh = constant.
- Faster flow, lower pressure.
- Atomisers, carburettors and the shower curtain that sucks inward all use it.
> But it isn't the whole story of how wings work

myth 4 F | Wings lift because air over the top must travel further to "catch up" with air underneath.
= There's no rule that the two streams must meet at the trailing edge. In fact the top air arrives *earlier*.
= Wings work by turning air downward (Newton's third law), with lower pressure above (consistent with Bernoulli).
= Planes can fly upside down: the angle of attack matters.
> Next: physics from accelerating frames

## p1-nonin | Non-inertial frames | physics | 2 | phys1 | p1-fluid
explain 3 | Fictitious forces appear in accelerating frames
- In a braking bus, you lurch forward. No force pushed you: the bus decelerated under you.
- In the bus's frame, physics works if you add a fictitious force −ma.
- Centrifugal and Coriolis forces are fictitious forces of rotating frames.
> Coriolis steers hurricanes

real 4 | Coriolis spins hurricanes, not bathtubs
- On a rotating Earth, moving air is deflected: right in the Northern Hemisphere, left in the Southern.
- Hurricanes rotate anticlockwise in the north, clockwise in the south.
- *Bathtubs and sinks are far too small:* their drain direction depends on how the water was poured.
> Foucault proved Earth rotates with a pendulum

story 4 | Foucault's pendulum, Paris 1851
- A 67 m pendulum in the Panthéon swung in a plane that slowly rotated.
- At latitude λ, the plane turns 360° in 24 h / sin λ: about 32 hours in Paris.
- *The first direct proof of Earth's rotation without looking at the sky.*
> Next: Lagrangian mechanics in practice

## p1-lagp | Lagrangian practice | physics | 3 | phys1 | p1-adv
quiz 3 | Lagrangian of a simple pendulum (length l, angle θ)?
* ½ml²θ̇² + mgl cos θ
o ½ml²θ̇² − mgl cos θ
o ½mθ̇² + mgθ
o ml²θ̇ + mgl sin θ
= T = ½ml²θ̇². With height measured from the pivot, V = −mgl cos θ, so L = T − V.
> Plug it into Euler–Lagrange

quiz 4 | What equation of motion does that Lagrangian give?
* θ̈ = −(g/l) sin θ
o θ̈ = −(g/l) θ
o θ̈ = (g/l) cos θ
o θ̈ = −g sin θ
= ∂L/∂θ̇ = ml²θ̇, its time derivative ml²θ̈. ∂L/∂θ = −mgl sin θ.
= Small angles: sin θ ≈ θ gives SHM.
> Cyclic coordinates give conserved quantities for free

explain 4 | Cyclic coordinates
- If L doesn't depend on a coordinate q, then ∂L/∂q = 0.
- Euler–Lagrange then says the momentum p = ∂L/∂q̇ is conserved.
- *A free particle in polar coordinates: θ is cyclic, so angular momentum is conserved.*
? What's conserved if L doesn't depend on coordinate q? :: Its conjugate momentum ∂L/∂q̇
> Constraints disappear when you choose good coordinates

explain 4 | Why Lagrangians make constraints easy
- A bead on a wire, a pendulum, a rolling cylinder: Newton needs the constraint forces.
- Lagrange uses coordinates that already obey the constraints: θ for the pendulum, not x and y.
- *The constraint forces never appear.* (Use multipliers if you want them.)
> Next: planets, properly

## p1-cent | Central forces | physics | 3 | phys1 | p1-lagp
explain 4 | The two-body problem reduces to one body
- Two masses orbiting each other = one particle of reduced mass μ = m₁m₂/(m₁ + m₂) moving in the same force.
- Angular momentum conservation keeps the orbit in a plane.
- An effective potential V(r) + L²/(2μr²) adds a centrifugal barrier.
> Inverse-square forces give closed ellipses

story 5 | Bertrand's theorem: only two forces give closed orbits
- For bound orbits to close for every starting condition, the force must be inverse square (planets) or linear (springs).
- Any other central force makes orbits precess, tracing rosettes.
- *Mercury's orbit precesses slightly too much for Newton.* General relativity accounts for the excess 43″ per century.
? Which central forces give closed orbits for all bound initial conditions? :: Inverse-square and linear (Hooke) forces
> Orbits are conic sections

quiz 3 | A planet's orbit has total energy E < 0. What shape is it?
* An ellipse (or circle)
o A parabola
o A hyperbola
o A spiral
= E < 0: bound ellipse. E = 0: parabola (just escapes). E > 0: hyperbola.
> Next: many masses and springs

## p1-norm | Normal modes | physics | 3 | phys1 | p1-cent
explain 4 | Normal modes: coupled oscillators untangled
- Two pendulums joined by a spring can swing in step or in opposition.
- Each is a normal mode with its own frequency. Any motion is a mix of the two.
- Finding them is an eigenvalue problem: det(K − ω²M) = 0.
> Coupled pendulums swap energy back and forth

predict 4 | Two identical pendulums are weakly coupled by a spring. You start one swinging. What happens?
* The energy slowly passes to the other and back again
o Both quickly swing together
o The second never moves
= Starting one pendulum excites both normal modes. Their slightly different frequencies beat.
= The motion transfers back and forth at the beat frequency.
> Molecules vibrate in normal modes too

real 4 | CO₂ absorbs infrared because of a normal mode
- CO₂ has a symmetric stretch, an asymmetric stretch and bending modes.
- The asymmetric stretch and bending modes change the dipole moment, so they absorb infrared radiation.
- *That's the greenhouse effect at molecular scale.*
> Next: rigid bodies in 3D

## p1-rigid | Rigid bodies in 3D | physics | 4 | phys1 | p1-norm
explain 4 | The inertia tensor
- In 3D, L = Iω where I is a 3×3 matrix. L and ω need not be parallel.
- Every body has three principal axes where they are parallel.
- *A wobbling frisbee or a badly balanced tyre: L and ω misaligned.*
> Spinning about the middle axis is unstable

story 5 | The tennis racket theorem
- A body spins stably about its largest and smallest principal axes but not the intermediate one.
- Throw a phone or tennis racket spinning around the middle axis and it flips over halfway.
- *Astronaut Vladimir Dzhanibekov filmed a wingnut flipping repeatedly in orbit in 1985.*
> Euler's equations describe the wobble

explain 5 | Euler's equations for rigid bodies
- $ I₁ω̇₁ = (I₂ − I₃)ω₂ω₃ (and cyclic)
- Linearise around spin on each axis: the middle axis gives exponential growth of small wobbles.
- That's the tennis racket instability, from three coupled ODEs.
> Physics 1 done. Physics 2: electricity and magnetism
